Lyntin has a series of commands that have been registered with the client
to enable you to change client side things.  For instance, there are
Lyntin commands for creating a session to a mud, creating aliases, removing
aliases, manipulating variables, so on and so forth.

Commands in Lyntin are denoted by the command character.  By default, this
character is "#" but it can be changed using the #config command and 
changing the commandchar.

There are some other special things you can do using the command 
character.

1. You can execute commands in other sessions by typing 

	#<SESSION_NAME> <COMMAND...>

2. You can execute commands in all sessions by typing 

	#all <COMMAND...>

3. You can execute a command a number of times by typing 

	#<NUMBER> <COMMAND...>

see also: semicolon

category: manual
